Campaign clip-seam-viewmodel, over Private/UI/FlowVizClipViewModel.cpp with
filter FlowViz. BEFORE (2026-08-06, 6 arms) and AFTER the stale-tail gap was
closed (after = 3-arm re-run 07:46-07:48Z against 435054e in
wind-worktrees/dispatchobs, same file, same filter):

    before    after     arm
    --------  --------  ----------------------------------------------
    killed    killed    count written as the PLANE count, not ENABLED
    SURVIVED  killed    unused tail left stale                <-- THE GAP
    killed    --        plane distance dropped
    killed    --        crop box left in SOLVER units
    killed    --        no-domain guard removed
    SURVIVED  SURVIVED  IDENTITY CONTROL                       (correct)

    before:  killed 4  SURVIVED 2  INVALID 0  UNSCORED 0  skipped 0
    after:   killed 2  SURVIVED 1  INVALID 0  UNSCORED 0  skipped 0   (3 arms)

The re-run carried three arms, not one, because an UNSCORED or mis-scored re-run
of a single arm proves nothing on its own: the gap arm, one ALREADY-KILLED
sibling to show the harness still kills, and the identity control to show it
still lets a no-op through. Both controls landed as expected, so the flip is
readable. Repo memory rerun-unknowns-with-controls. Baseline green before
scoring, 106/106 enumerated tests completed in every arm.

WHICH ASSERTIONS KILLED WHICH ARM, because a KILLED only says the suite went
red, and here two arms broke the same struct field family:

    tail arm     FlowViz.UI.ClipViewModel.Consumer, SIX assertions, all of them
                 added by this task:
                   'exactly three slots are non-zero ...'  expected 3, got 6
                   'the unused tail is zeroed ...'         expected 2, got 6
                   'slot 2/3/4/5 is exactly zero after the apply ...'

    count arm    FlowViz.Render.ClipSeam ('a disabled plane is not counted',
                 'the count DROPS back to one') AND
                 FlowViz.UI.ClipViewModel.Consumer ('two planes now reach the
                 shader')

Different tests, different assertions, no overlap. So the two arms are told
apart by what they broke rather than by their position in the list.

GOT 6, WHICH IS THE MECHANISM AND NOT JUST A FAILURE. Six is every slot still
holding the poison plane. Under the OLD fixture those same slots read 0 -- not
because the apply cleared them, but because FlowVizRayMarch::FillDefaults had
zeroed all six a few lines earlier. That is precisely why the arm survived.

THE GAP -- AN ASSERTION THAT ALREADY EXISTED AND COULD NOT FAIL.

This was not a missing test. FlowVizClipViewModelTest.cpp already carried

    TestEqual(TEXT("the unused tail is zeroed, so a later count raise cannot "
                   "resurrect a removed plane"), CountNonZeroPlanes(Reduced), 2);

directly under a comment explaining why the zeroed tail matters. It reads as
thorough coverage. It cannot fail: the block came from FillDefaults, which
zeroes all six slots, so the assertion was reading FillDefaults' zeros and
attributing them to ApplyToRayMarchParameters. Delete the production tail loop
and the test still passes.

THE FIX IS THE FIXTURE, NOT THE ASSERTION. PoisonEverySlot writes a real,
valid, clearly-authored plane (+Y normal, offset 99) into ALL SIX slots before
the apply. That converts the question from 'is the tail absent' -- which was
already true -- into 'did the apply ERASE what was there', which is the property
the shader actually depends on. Repo memory accumulators-need-a-second-write:
a write is indistinguishable from a no-op unless something else was there first.
A CONTROL asserts the poison landed (all six slots non-zero) before the apply
runs, so a poison helper that silently did nothing could not make the tail
assertion pass by default.

NOT (0,0,0,-1) AS THE POISON, though it is the most destructive value a slot can
hold. That value is already the discriminator the IsSlotInert fixture is built
on, and reusing it would leave a failure ambiguous between 'the tail was not
cleared' and 'the helper mis-classifies'. A poison must be distinguishable from
every other thing the test is trying to prove.

BOTH SITES WERE FIXED, NOT JUST THE ONE THE ARM NAMED. The same file asserts
'exactly three slots are non-zero' over a freshly-defaulted block a few lines
above, with the identical defect for the identical reason. The arm only pointed
at one of them. Repo memory a-finding-names-an-instance: grep the pattern, do
not patch the coordinates.

WHY IT MATTERS, and why it is not merely theoretical. NumClipPlanes is itself a
written value -- the FIRST arm of this campaign proves a mutant can write the
wrong count. Compose the two: a wrong count plus a stale tail is a plane the
user deleted, silently clipping the image, with no error anywhere. Each defect
alone is caught; together the tail turns an off-by-one count into a visibly
wrong picture that still looks like a picture.

THE SEAM TEST'S TWO SIMILAR CLAIMS WERE CHECKED AND LEFT ALONE.
FlowVizClipSeamTest.cpp asserts 'the disabled plane's slot is zeroed, not left
stale' and 'slot 1 is zeroed on the way down'. Those cannot be poisoned the same
way: the parameter block is built INSIDE the dispatcher and only copied out
through PeekRequestParameters, so the test never owns it before the write. They
still do useful work -- they catch a disabled plane being WRITTEN to slot 1 --
they simply cannot speak for the tail-clearing loop. The view model test owns
its block, which is why the close lives there.

Arm order in the re-run file is gap-first, then the killed sibling, then the
control. The identity control must SURVIVE; if it ever does not, the harness is
scoring noise and every line above is void.

The rationale lives here rather than at the top of the .txt because
parse_mutants.py splits on `%%` and raises ValueError on any record that is not
exactly three `--`-separated fields. A prose header would abort the campaign.

Tracked as task #56. Re-run file: clip-seam-viewmodel-tailrerun.txt.
